    contentEn: `<p>Stepping off a train at Granada's station on Avenida de Andaluces and heading to NH Collection Granada Victoria on Puerta Real is a short journey — about 3 kilometres and roughly 10 minutes by car — but arriving after a rail journey, sometimes involving a change of train, is exactly the moment a pre-arranged private transfer is most useful. Rather than hauling luggage to the taxi rank or trying to work out the right bus after what may have been a long day of travel, your driver waits just outside the station with a name board and takes you straight to the hotel's entrance on Puerta Real.</p>
<p>NH Collection Granada Victoria's location right on the square makes it one of the simplest drop-offs in the city centre, and a driver who knows the one-way system around Puerta Real can usually pull up close to the entrance even during busier periods. For guests arriving after Granada's single direct daily train from Madrid, or after connecting through Antequera-Santa Ana, that directness is worth more than the short distance suggests, since it removes one more decision point at the end of what has often already been a multi-leg journey.</p>
<p>Booking your pickup through <a href="/get-a-quote/">get a quote</a> in advance means your driver already knows your train number and expected arrival time, so there's no need to call ahead or hope a taxi happens to be waiting when you step off the platform.</p>
<h2>About NH Collection Granada Victoria</h2>
<p>NH Collection Granada Victoria sits on Puerta Real, the square where Gran Vía de Colón, Calle Reyes Católicos, and Acera del Darro converge, making it about as central a base as Granada offers. From here, the Cathedral, the Capilla Real, and the Alcaicería's narrow former silk-market lanes are all within easy walking distance, and the wider historic centre — including the lower reaches of the Albaicín and the Realejo — is accessible on foot for guests who prefer to explore without a car once they've settled in.</p>
<p>Because the hotel sits directly on one of Granada's busiest squares, arrival by private transfer avoids any uncertainty about which entrance to use or how to navigate the surrounding one-way streets with a suitcase in tow — your driver simply pulls up at the right spot and helps unload before you check in.</p>
<h3>Arriving After a Connecting Train</h3>
<p>If your journey to Granada involved a change of train at Antequera-Santa Ana, or any other connection, arrival times can shift slightly depending on how smoothly that connection went. A private transfer that tracks your train, or that you've briefed in advance about a flexible arrival window, adapts far more easily than trying to arrange transport reactively once you're already standing on the platform with your bags.</p>
<h3>A Central First Base in Granada</h3>
<p>For travellers whose first proper stop in Granada is NH Collection Granada Victoria, arriving by private transfer rather than working out public transport gives you a calmer introduction to a city whose historic core takes some getting used to. Being dropped directly on Puerta Real, rather than at a bus stop some distance away, means your very first steps in Granada are toward your hotel entrance rather than a search for the right street — a small detail that matters more than it sounds after a train journey, particularly if it's your first visit to the city.</p>
<h3>Settling in Before You Explore the Centre</h3>
<p>Once you've checked in, Puerta Real's position puts the Cathedral, the Alcaicería, and the wider shopping district all within a short walk, so many guests use their first afternoon simply to get their bearings before tackling the steeper walk up to the Alhambra or the Albaicín on a later day. Arriving without the stress of navigating public transport from the station leaves more energy for that first walk, rather than spending it on logistics before you've even unpacked.</p>
<h3>Why the Square's Layout Rewards a Private Pickup</h3>
<p>Puerta Real sees a genuine mix of pedestrian and vehicle traffic throughout the day, and the one-way system routing cars around the square can confuse a driver seeing it for the first time, whether that's a taxi driver unfamiliar with the specific stretch or a rental car navigated by a jet-lagged traveller. A chauffeur who works this route regularly already knows which approach gets closest to the hotel entrance at any given hour, turning what could be a confusing final few hundred metres into a non-issue.</p>
<h3>A Simple Start to a Longer Andalusian Trip</h3>
<p>For many travellers, Granada is one stop on a wider Andalusian itinerary rather than the only destination, and NH Collection Granada Victoria's central position makes it a practical base from which to plan the rest of the trip. Arriving smoothly, without the friction of an unfamiliar bus route or a confusing walk from the station, sets a good tone for everything that follows, whether the next stop is a day trip into the Alhambra, an excursion to Sierra Nevada, or onward travel to another Andalusian city entirely. A calm, well-organised first hour in Granada tends to set the tone for the rest of a multi-stop trip in a way that's easy to underestimate before you've actually experienced the alternative.</p>
<h2>Practical Info for This Transfer</h2>
<p>Your driver meets you just outside the station entrance on Avenida de Andaluces, helps with luggage, and drives directly to Puerta Real — a short trip that rarely takes more than 10 minutes even allowing for the square's daytime traffic. If you've arrived on Granada's one direct daily train from Madrid, the journey itself takes around three hours and fifty minutes via Loja, Antequera, and Córdoba, so a smooth final leg to the hotel is a welcome way to finish a fairly long day of travel.</p>
<p>Families and small groups can travel together in a single vehicle sized for everyone's luggage, which is particularly convenient if your journey to Granada involved a change of train and you'd rather not manage bags through yet another unassisted step. For guests who want to continue exploring Andalusia after settling in, our <a href="/hourly-chauffeur/">hourly chauffeur</a> service can be arranged directly from the hotel, and our <a href="/granada/">Granada</a> page has more on the city itself. If your itinerary includes other cities reached by road rather than rail, our <a href="/city-to-city-transfers/">city-to-city transfers</a> service is worth considering as an alternative to Granada's more limited train timetable.</p>`,
